Review 1:
The points made for there to be a unified "Discussion" section are valid, but that would arguably only lead to a refactor of the paper as it currently stands, which could very well be interpreted as a personal opinion rather than an objective read on the paper's structure.
The main findings are well documented under "III. Results", so there should be no need to solely nest it under a separate section.
The comparison to the literature is mentioned in the introduction (page two, second paragraph), indicating concisely how the paper's implementation stands out from the other existing two. This is referenced again in the results section with figure 10, which points out the throughput differences between the implementations.
The strengths and limitations, as per my understanding, are sequentially built throughout the results, culminating in the conclusion: the network bottleneck appearing in the GPU executions, only to be mitigated through simulating more powerful bandwidths.
Lastly, the practical implications could indeed be explored further, as there is no mention of the Fletcher's method's application in petroleum exploration mentioned in the introduction.
Most of what has been mentioned is well documented in the paper, leading to the comments being suggestions based on structural preferences rather than objective lack of information.

Review 2:
The introduction could very well be split in two, explicitly highlighting the related work. Same could be said for the numerical validation part. These are valid points.
The comparison with the state of the art is briefly mentioned in the introduction (as noted by "this is the first implementation that can be used in several nodes"), and it is also mentioned in the results in figure 10: the "single-node" source is one of the other state-of-the-art implementations, thus why it was compared to the one introduced by the paper. It might have, however, lacked clarity in pointing this data as coming from an existing implementation.

Review 3:
The mathematical operations executed by the Fletcher application are homogeneous and extremely simple across the domain. In this case, the computational gain from dynamic partitioning in heterogeneous nodes would be mitigated by the simplicity of the operations when compared to their computational cost for the GPU. However, nodes with extreme heterogeneity could still be highly affected by simple static partitioning indeed.
In regards to "the extra cells are never updated by the stencil”: the domain is always extended with the worker's immediate neighbours' halos, which are updated at each time step. These extra cells are not, however, updated through computation (they are not part of the worker's workload, thus, they are "ghost" cells).
In 2B, there could be some code blocks indeed to make this section more "code-oriented". However, it is arguable whether it has practically the same information as A: this section describes the application flow, including its initialization and core computation loop (stencil), highlighting the advantages of the distributed implementation. All the information present in B is from the point of view of the implementation, closely addressing MPI concepts. Section A, although it briefly mentions MPI, it focuses on core HPC theory: fletcher modeling and partitioning. It is indeed arguable if the sentence "In practice, this 3D partition..." belongs to the theoretical background, though.
In III. Results, the "synthetic task" refers to the fact that the program has an MPI tracing library attached to it, meaning that a clear record of all communications/MPI calls is available for every run. These events, however, do not accomodate "computation", since they are MPI-only. Thus, computation times are inferred based on the application flow - computation always happens right after an MPI_Isend call. Bearing this in mind, it is possible to create a "synthetic task" for computation by following the aforementioned logic.
When it comes to the impracticalities of the increase of the block size, that refers specifically to the environment the experiments ran in: the available bandwidth constraints restricted experimentation, thus the path to investigate the application's characteristics further was not to enlarge the domain. In this scenario, the simulation tool acts as a mean to find the ideal cluster characteristics for the program to run in, which, in our context, meant improving the network capabilities. It is noteworthy, however, that as a distributed application, overheads are always introduced, so an analysis on whether it would be computationally worth it to scale the application on a specific cluster is always warranted.
In relation to figure 10, the difference being mentioned is the one between "Simulation - 100Gbps 8 workers" and "Simulation - 10Gbps 5 workers". The discrepancy is indeed striking, as the former scales much more intensely than the latter. The discussion on deriving the minimal number of workers is an interesting future work, indeed.

Review 4:
The research begins as a distributed implementation following well-known practices on a popular application within seismic propagation, but evolves into a performance analysis with calibrated simulation, shifting its potential from simply distributing the Fletcher method. As noted, further investigation of this trend could represent an interesting topic for future work.
